 for their well-being for their  uh for their health for their education  by being in schools and again that's why  we have the twice weekly testing and  it's why we have all the raft of  control measures within the school  premises to minimize the risk of  transmission of the virus  um yeah it's not working though is it 93  000 young people have failed to return  to full-time  education after schools reopen their  doors um  what are you going to do about that well  this is an issue that's always worried  us  um that's about 30 000 more than you see  in a normal non-pandemic  year these are vulnerable children we  want them in school  when the lockdown occurred in march we  took extra measures to  get schools to talk to the parents of of  those disengaged  children who were not coming into school  schools remained open of course if you  remember for  vulnerable children and the children of  critical workers where we weren't seeing  those families sending their children  into school  uh the school would contact the families  we would work with our regional teams  uh local authority level to again to  talk to those families via  via social workers and so on to help do  everything we can to  encourage those children to to come into  school  perhaps extending the school day by half  an hour is that going to happen from  september  well catch up of course is very  important even as early as  june last year uh the prime minister  announced a billion pounds of funding  for catch up for money to schools to  spend as they felt  appropriate to help children to catch up  also the national tutoring program was  introduced  uh back in june last year um and  we've uh earlier in june we announced  another 1.4 billion bringing the total  together with a february announcement up  to over three billion pounds of catch up  uh and that includes money also to help  uh  to to for teachers to do the to be the  best that they can through extra  cpd and training because we know that  the best catch-up of course k happens  every day in school with children at  school in those classrooms  um but we're also conducting a review uh  right now of the evidence about  extending the school day and  time spent in school to understand how  that would work  how effective it would be uh to if we  were to increase the  the length of the school day okay the  other thing you're looking at is mobile  free  school days i mean schools already have  very robust policies  in place when it comes to the use of  mobiles  they know what they're doing don't they  a number of schools increasing number of  schools are  banning mobile phones from the school  day keeping them out of the classrooms  they are a distraction there is  evidence that that they improve  entertainment particularly  uh children of the you know less  academic children  do better if they're not distracted by  mobile phones countries like france  banned  mobile phones from schools two or three  years ago  and i've always been in favor of banning  mobile phones from the classroom they  also can affect children's  mental health and their well-being  they're continually spending you know  too much time  on the screen in our uh  curriculum on relationship and health  education  uh we you know we ask teachers to teach  children  the importance of rationing the use of  mobile phones so they are spending time  outside with friends doing their  homework talking to their parents and  not always glued  to their mobile phone so yes we we do  want to to ban  mobile phones from the classroom but we  are today  launching a call for evidence to see how  best to implement such a policy  and also other measures we want to take  to ensure that we eliminate  low-level disruption in our classroom  get rid of bullying in our classrooms we  want children to have a happy  safe time at school and building on the  work we've  conducted over the last 10 years to give  teachers more  power more control over the classroom  this is going a step further now to make  sure that every classroom is safe  and a happy place you know the teachers  unions have said that they already do  that so they're not quite sure why  you're  why you're saying that this morning  something that else that you had  said that people that pupils don't need  from the 17th of may was masks  in schools and as a result we've seen  the number of kids that have tested  positive  going up and up and up will you look at  that again come september will kids have  to potentially wear masks when they go  back to school  well first of all increasing uh  numbers of children who are isolating  the three percent dimension earlier  is driven mainly by infection rates in  the community there's a correlation  between  the number of children who are asked to  self-isolate because they've come into  contact with somebody who've covered  and the degree of infection in the the  community  we introduced the face coverings for  pupils in secondary school classrooms  uh as a temporary measure we reviewed  that over easter  and from may the 17th it's no longer a  recommendation but of course  we do give discretion to directors of  public health around the country  particularly in areas of high infection  rates  to re to reintroduce or to maintain that  recommendation  uh in classrooms and i think in about 60  local authorities  in those high infection rates around the  country that there are recommendations  to continue  uh face coverings in in classrooms but  it was a temporary measure  uh and as i said the the the issue isn't  about infections  within the school of a huge raft of  measures in place so hygiene ventilation  cycle breaks and so on  to limit the risk of transmission in  schools  this is about transmission in the  community um will you be wearing a mask  after the 19th of july  indoors well i i wear a mask  uh now when uh when when necessary  and that's the law yes and i will um  we'll see what is announced uh as we  move to step four  